{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC5/AC6 — sweep for the `environ.get(KEY, \"false\") == \"true\"` shape, with dispositions\n\nCommand (re-runnable, named in the plan):\n\n```\ngrep -rn --include='*.py' -E 'environ\\.get\\([^)]*\\)\\s*==\\s*\"true\"' scripts/ src/ infra/\ngrep -rn --include='*.py' -E '==\\s*\"true\"' scripts/ src/ infra/        # widened net\ngrep -rn \"steps\\..*\\.outputs\\.\" .github/workflows/*.yml | grep -E \"==|!=\"  # YAML twin\n```\n\n**The exact shape (`.get(KEY, \"false\") == \"true\"`) has exactly ONE instance repo-wide** —\nthe defect. Everything else below came from the widened nets.\n\n### Same family (an env var fed from a CI step output gates a destructive arm)\n\n| Site | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| `scripts/dependency_audit.py:488` `BLOCKING` | **FIXED** — this ticket. Tri-state; absent/empty/unrecognised refuses with exit 1. |\n| `scripts/canary_bridge.py:401,406,416,456` `STALE` | **SAFE** — reads `stale`, then branches `== \"true\"` … `elif stale == \"false\" and tid`. `\"\"` matches NEITHER arm, so a crashed check falls through to a no-op and cannot close. This is the tri-state the fix adopts. |\n| `scripts/canary_bridge.py:601,607,619,641` `DRIFT_FOUND` | **SAFE**, doubly. Same explicit tri-state; and its producing step (`Check bridge binding-drift`, `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:199`) is **not** `continue-on-error`, so a crash fails the job before the alert step is reached at all. |\n| `.github/workflows/dependency-advisory-canary.yml:183` `if: steps.audit.outputs.blocking == 'true'` | **NOTED — the YAML twin of the same defect, now MITIGATED.** On a crash this is `''`, so the \"Fail the run\" step does not fire and the run would conclude success. That is exactly AC2. It is mitigated in this change without weakening `continue-on-error`: the escalation step is not `continue-on-error`, so `advisory-alert`'s exit 1 reddens the run. A comment at the audit step records the coupling so it is not silently broken later. |\n| `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:226` `if: steps.check.outputs.stale == 'true'` | **NOTED, out of family.** Same YAML shape, but its only arm is \"fail the run\" — a missing output loses a red signal, it does not destroy a ticket. No destructive arm, so it is not this defect's class. Left alone deliberately rather than swept up. |\n\n### Same textual shape, NOT the same family\n\n| Site | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| `scripts/dependency_audit.py:483`, `canary_bridge.py:396,598` `DRY_RUN == \"true\"` | **SAFE** — fails in the safe direction. An absent `DRY_RUN` means \"not a dry run\", i.e. the lane does its normal job. It cannot cause a close that would not otherwise happen. |\n| `scripts/normalize_ci_conclusion.py:30` `failure_observed == \"true\"` | **SAFE** — reachable only when `conclusion == \"skipped\"`, and the function's documented posture is the one this fix adopts: *\"Empty or any unrecognized value: fail closed.\"* |\n| `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:547` | **SAFE** — compares `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` **stdout**, not an env var; best-effort with a documented fallthrough. |\n| `scripts/check_wall_clock_asserts.py:25,57` | **N/A** — docstring prose quoting the `os.environ.get(\"CI\") == \"true\"` idiom; not executable. |\n| `src/rebar/_bridge_runner.py:105`, `src/rebar/_commands/_init_ensures.py:136` | **SAFE** — subprocess stdout comparisons (`git config`), not CI step outputs. |\n| `src/rebar/llm/evals/eval_scorers.py:105` | **SAFE** — compares an eval fixture dict field. Unrelated. |\n\n**Conclusion.** The destructive form of this pattern was a one-off; the two sibling bot\nalert lanes had already adopted the tri-state, which is why this change aligns the\noutlier with them rather than inventing a new posture. The one genuinely open item is the\nYAML twin's general form, which is why the workflow now carries a comment tying the audit\nstep's `continue-on-error` to the escalation step's exit-1 behaviour."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788588956273691001,"uuid":"2cdf4670-3357-4572-b1a1-fa0bd22dd77b"}